186361108 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362091 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186361122 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362091 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186361267 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362091 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186361332 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362091 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186361508 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362091 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362091 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362091 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186361508 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362091 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "832aff61-5879-5808-944b-77a06173542d" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362094 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186361085 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362094 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186361325 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362094 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186361451 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362094 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186361744 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362094 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186361786 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362094 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362094 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362094 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186361085 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362094 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "85184abf-390f-5ccf-a8a9-0834b6bdbc7a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362298 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186362091 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362298 #edb/ref 1069 "q-98525" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362298 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362298 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following is the phase line of the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}={y}^{2}+y-2?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362298 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "1f99c9bb-9f0b-5721-8c35-4c71f8216676" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186362298 #edb/ref 1074 "To draw the phase line of the differential equation, we proceed using the following three steps:\n\n**Step 1**: Find the equilibrium solutions of the differential equation. \n\nIn this case, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}+y-2 & =0 \\\\ (y-1)(y+2) & =0 \\\\ y & =-2,1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the equilibrium solutions are $y=-2$ and $y=1.$\n\n**Step 2**: Find the intervals of $y$ for which $f(y)>0$ and $f(y)<0.$ \n\nConstructing a sign table, we get the following:\n\n|  | $(-\\infty ,-2)$ | $(-2,1)$ | $(1,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y+2$ | $-$ | $+$ | $+$ |\n| $y-1$ | $-$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n| $f(y)$ | $+$ | $-$ | $+$ |\n\nSo, we have that $f(y)$ is positive for $y\\in (-\\infty ,-2)\\cup (1,\\infty ),$ and negative for $y\\in (-2,1).$\n\n**Step 3**: Draw the phase line using the following recipe:\n\n- Draw a vertical $y$-axis\n\n- Mark the equilibrium solutions\n\n- Draw arrows pointing up in the intervals where $f(y)>0$\n\n- Draw arrows pointing down in the intervals where $f(y)<0$\n\nApplying the above recipe, we get the following phase line for our differential equation.\n\n![](images/39/39ab4bfad5e05067d9e50979deb2a7699eb41aa87037fdf6e3a6d14a98dcfa19.png)" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186365253 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186365254 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186365253 #edb/ref 1069 "q-163548" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186365253 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186365253 #edb/ref 1071 "A particle $P$ moves along the $x$-axis in the direction of increasing $x$ where $x\\ge 0.$ When the displacement of $P$ from the origin is $x\\,m,$ the velocity is $v\\,m/s,$ and the acceleration is $(4-x)\\,{m/s}^{2}.$ If $P$ has velocity $v=6\\,m/s$ at the origin, find the maximum velocity of the particle." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186365253 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "c0d1d1a5-d9cb-5570-acc7-da72657b9a2b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186365253 #edb/ref 1074 "![](images/d4/d4e5b78932f8ff6155ac81e12538eb31ac211c9d27d6a2b0c67542527ccd3b2a.png)\n\nThe extrema of the velocity occur at the critical points of $v.$ Since the acceleration of the particle $P$ is $(4-x)\\,{m/s}^{2},$ we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=4-x.\n$$\n\nTo find the moments where $v$ is a maximum, we need to solve $\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=0$ and test each solution using the first or second derivative test.\n\nSolving $\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=0$ gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4-x & =0 \\\\ x & =4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, the extremum of the velocity function occurs when $x=4\\,m.$\n\nWe now test the extreme value using the first derivative test. We summarize the necessary information in a table, shown below.\n\n| $x$ | $(0,4)$ | $4$ | $(4,\\infty )$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| Test Point | $2$ |  | $6$ |\n| $\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}$ | $2>0$ | $0$ | $-2<0$ |\n| $v$ | $↗$ | max | $↘$ |\n\nWe conclude that the velocity is maximized when $x=4\\,m.$\n\nNext, using the fact that\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}),\n$$\n\nwe can rewrite our initial equation as\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})=4-x.\n$$\n\nIntegrating both sides of the above equation with respect to $x,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\int \\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}x}(\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2})\\text{d}x=\\int (4-x)\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nwhich gives the general solution\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2}=4x-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}+C.\n$$\n\nNow, since $v=6\\,m/s$ when $x=0\\,m,$ we can find the value of the constant $C,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =4x-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}+C \\\\ \\frac{1}{2}(6)^{2} & =4(0)-\\frac{1}{2}(0)^{2}+C \\\\ C & =18\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{1}{2}{v}^{2} & =4x-\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}+18 \\\\ {v}^{2} & =8x-{x}^{2}+36 \\\\ v & =\\pm \\sqrt{-{x}^{2}+8x+36}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the particle is moving in the direction of increasing $x,$ we have that $v(x)>0,$ so we can disregard the negative square root. Therefore,\n\n$$\nv(x)=\\sqrt{-{x}^{2}+8x+36}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we calculate the maximum velocity:\n\n$$\n{v}_{max}=v(4)=\\sqrt{-(4)^{2}+8(4)+36}=2\\sqrt{13}\\,m/s\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186365254 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186365255 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186365254 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186365256 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186365254 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186365257 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186365254 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186365258 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186365254 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186365259 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186365254 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186365254 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186365254 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186365258 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186365254 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "95c44088-63d3-5d47-8f73-8487757bc6ec" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186365255 #edb/ref 1080 "8\\,m/s" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186365255 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186365255 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "ec26c32f-330e-5878-8370-d35956d6bd96" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186365256 #edb/ref 1080 "6\\,m/s" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186365256 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186365256 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "255624ce-60b8-5c54-9dfe-53912d1536ec" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186365257 #edb/ref 1080 "4\\sqrt{3}\\,m/s" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186365257 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186365257 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "fe85ed8c-5a74-5ea1-9034-77b240f6eb4b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186365258 #edb/ref 1080 "2\\sqrt{13}\\,m/s" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186365258 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186365258 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "3a46d353-568f-565f-a23b-bcdc3c812f55" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186365259 #edb/ref 1080 "3\\sqrt{6}\\,m/s" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186365259 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17