7592186045417] [17592186375555 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186375555 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "b0642d18-3c15-5486-bd57-17e7c441a97b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186375556 #edb/ref 1080 "-4<x\\le 14" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186375556 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186375556 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "935a8f0a-e2df-59b4-b4ca-77e1fae1f757" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186385178 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186385179 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186385178 #edb/ref 1069 "q-334241" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186385178 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186385178 #edb/ref 1071 "For which of the following values is the implicit Euler method stable, given that $\\lambda $ is the parameter of the test equation?\n\nI. $\\lambda Δx=0.1$\n\nII. $\\lambda Δx=0.8$\n\nIII. $\\lambda Δx=1.2$\n\nIV. $\\lambda Δx=5$\n\n*Hint: The stability function $R$ of the implicit Euler method is given by*\n\n$$\nR(\\lambda Δx)=\\frac{1}{1+\\lambda Δx}.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186385178 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d7019503-fd19-5657-a7e0-238936f56d67" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186385178 #edb/ref 1074 "A numerical method \n${y}_{n+1}={y}_{n}+Δ{y}_{n}$\nis stable for a given step size $Δx$ if, when applied to the test equation\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}=-\\lambda y,\\,y(0)=1,\\,x\\ge 0,\\,\\lambda >0,\n$$\n\nthe numerical solution satisfies ${y}_{n}\\to 0$ as $n\\to \\infty ,$ just like the exact solution.\n\nWhen a method is applied to the test equation, the update takes the form\n\n$$\n{y}_{n+1}=R(\\lambda Δx){y}_{n},\n$$\n\nwhere $R(\\lambda Δx)$ is referred to as the stability function of the method. The set of values for which the method is stable, given by $|R(\\lambda Δx)|<1,$ is called the stability region of the method.\n\nThe stability function of the implicit Euler method is\n\n$$\nR(\\lambda Δx)=\\frac{1}{1+\\lambda Δx}.\n$$\n\nLet's check the stability of the implicit Euler Method for each of the given values in turn.\n\n- For $\\lambda Δx=0.1,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(0.1)| & =|\\frac{1}{1+0.1}| \\\\  & =|\\frac{1}{1.1}| \\\\  & =0.9090…<1.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this value falls *within* the stability region, the implicit Euler method is stable.\n\n- For $\\lambda Δx=0.8,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(0.8)| & =|\\frac{1}{1+0.8}| \\\\  & =|\\frac{1}{1.8}| \\\\  & =0.5555…<1.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this value falls *within* the stability region, the implicit Euler method is stable.\n\n- For $\\lambda Δx=1.2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(1.2)| & =|\\frac{1}{1+1.2}| \\\\  & =|\\frac{1}{2.2}| \\\\  & =0.4545…<1.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this value falls *within* the stability region, the implicit Euler method is stable.\n\n- For $\\lambda Δx=5,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|R(5)| & =|\\frac{1}{1+5}| \\\\  & =|\\frac{1}{6}| \\\\  & =0.1666…<1.\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince this value falls *within* the stability region, the implicit Euler method is stable.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, III, and IV\".\n\nNote that, since the implicit Euler method is absolutely stable, it is stable for all possible values of $\\lambda Δx.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186385179 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186385180 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186385179 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186385181 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186385179 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186385182 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186385179 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186385183 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186385179 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186385184 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186385179 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186385179 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186385179 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186385184 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186385179 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "1c6b2119-fae8-58ab-8eef-754e579be530" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186385180 #edb/re